% ============================================================================
% comet-part — three named parts of a comet and what each actually is
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A comet is not one solid lump: at its heart is a frozen nucleus, that
% nucleus sheds gas and dust into a fuzzy coma around it, and as the comet
% nears the Sun that gas and dust streams away into a bright tail. Recall
% is a binding query:
%
%     ? comet_part(nucleus, $D)
%       % solid_frozen_core_at_the_heart_of_the_comet
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `comet_part(part, description)` carrying the source citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on a comet-related term that is not one of these
% three. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the
% part it names:
%
%     ? comet_part($P, fuzzy_cloud_of_gas_and_dust_around_the_nucleus)
%       % coma
%
% The three comet parts, as a little truth table:
%
%     part      description                                                    quote (verbatim)
%     --------  -------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     nucleus    solid_frozen_core_at_the_heart_of_the_comet                    "At the heart of every comet is a solid, frozen core called the nucleus."
%     coma       fuzzy_cloud_of_gas_and_dust_around_the_nucleus                  "The gas and dust create a huge, fuzzy cloud around the nucleus called the coma."
%     tail       streams_away_from_the_nucleus_pushed_by_sunlight_and_solar_particles   "As dust and gases stream away from the nucleus, sunlight and particles coming from the Sun push them into a bright tail that stretches away from the Sun for millions of miles."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from NASA Space Place's "What Is a Comet?"
% page, which introduces each of these three physical parts in its own
% sentence before going on to further sub-divide the tail into a dust tail
% and an ion tail. The original authoring note said the quotes were
% "WebFetch-verified before writing (twice)"; the raw-page measurement
% below supersedes it. `trust authoritative` — NASA is a primary .gov
% source, the same tier the sibling `moon-phases.adj` and
% `solar-eclipse-type.adj` (the other libraries in this directory) already
% use for their NASA citations. A comet-related term that is NOT one of
% these three — "short_period_comet", a real classification the same page
% also names, but one that sorts comets by ORBITAL PERIOD rather than by
% physical anatomy, a different axis from nucleus/coma/tail — is
% deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing
% a physical-part description.
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SENTENCE (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The
% envelope used to be the nucleus span, so a coma or tail answer was
% warranted primarily by a sentence about the nucleus, and those two
% sentences reached no answer at all. Each row now carries the sentence
% introducing its own part, and the envelope is the page's sentence
% framing the anatomy as a whole, which names no part:
%
%     "This diagram is not to scale, but it shows the anatomy of a
%      comet."
%
% The three spans quoted in the truth table above ARE the row `source`
% values, so each description is checkable from the row that states it.
%
% NOTE FOR ANY FUTURE TEST: all three spans mention the nucleus, because
% the coma is a cloud AROUND the nucleus and the tail streams AWAY FROM
% it. A negative assertion must therefore name a WHOLE SPAN; the bare
% word `nucleus` is satisfied by all three.
%
% Measured 2026-09-16 UTC by this table's own fetch. The page answered
% 200 in about 21830 bytes and a nonsense path on the same host answered
% 404 with a full styled page of about 11280 bytes -- so the control is
% that the spans are absent from a page of COMPARABLE SIZE, not that the
% page is empty. Those byte counts are measured-at-a-moment, not
% properties of the page: two fetches minutes apart returned 21823 and
% 21830 bytes. Tags removed without a space and only ASCII whitespace
% collapsed: each row's span and the envelope each occur exactly once,
% inside one `<p>`, scripts set aside and over the whole file, with no
% `<head>` copy. Every row's page is the envelope's, so no row restates a
% `locator` line and no row repeats the `trust` tier.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table comet_part {
    columns part, description

    row (nucleus, solid_frozen_core_at_the_heart_of_the_comet) {
        source "At the heart of every comet is a solid, frozen core called the nucleus."
    }
    row (coma, fuzzy_cloud_of_gas_and_dust_around_the_nucleus) {
        source "The gas and dust create a huge, fuzzy cloud around the nucleus called the coma."
    }
    row (tail, streams_away_from_the_nucleus_pushed_by_sunlight_and_solar_particles) {
        source "As dust and gases stream away from the nucleus, sunlight and particles coming from the Sun push them into a bright tail that stretches away from the Sun for millions of miles."
    }

    source "This diagram is not to scale, but it shows the anatomy of a comet."
    locator "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/comets/en/"
    trust authoritative
}
